## PART B — KNOWLEDGE BASE TEMPLATE (paste into the agent's knowledge base)

Fill every field from the client intake. Leave nothing as a guess — if the client didn't provide it, omit it rather than inventing.

```
BUSINESS: [BUSINESS NAME]
TYPE: [BUSINESS TYPE]
LOCATION: [ADDRESS]
SERVICE AREA: [SERVICE AREA — e.g. "Las Vegas, Henderson, Summerlin"]
HOURS: [HOURS — full weekly schedule]
PHONE: [PHONE NUMBER]
WEBSITE: [WEBSITE URL]
BOOKING: [BOOKING URL / how appointments are scheduled]

SERVICES OFFERED:
[SERVICES LIST — each service with a one-line description]

PRICING GUIDANCE:
[PRICING — general ranges or "starting at" figures the agent may share.
If the business does free estimates or consultations, state it here. If
pricing should NOT be quoted by phone, write: "Do not quote prices — offer
to book a consultation / estimate instead."]

CURRENT OFFERS (factual, only mention if caller asks about specials):
[CURRENT OFFER or "None"]

GUARANTEES / POLICIES (only state what is listed here):
[GUARANTEES — e.g. satisfaction guarantee, warranty — or "None stated"]

APPOINTMENT TYPES & AVAILABILITY:
[APPOINTMENT TYPES — what can be booked, typical durations, whether
same-day / after-hours / emergency slots exist]

EMERGENCY DEFINITION (for this business):
[EMERGENCY DEFINITION — what counts as urgent and what the agent should do]

FREQUENTLY ASKED QUESTIONS:
[FAQ 1 — question + answer]
[FAQ 2 — question + answer]
[FAQ 3 — question + answer]
[Add as many as the client provides]

WHAT TO DO WITH UNKNOWN QUESTIONS:
Capture the caller's name and number and promise a team follow-up. Never
guess.
```

